seven years ago I couldn't stand  Assassin's Creed 3 its recent remaster  changed my mind upon its initial 2012  release Assassin's Creed 3 was divisive  to say the least  critically it was well received the  Metacritic score sits at 84 with  numerous publications calling it the  best Series entry to date they praised  its characters setting and wealth of  open-world activities if metacritic were  the sole indication of a games worth  Assassin's Creed 3 would have a  sparkling reputation but the other group  one to which I belong since 2012  told a much more tarnished story of a  game with technical issues and sloppy  mechanics a boring protagonist and  menial open-world quests it was a messy  conglomeration of action set-pieces that  never really meshed and as the series  continued its steady march I  consistently ranked it at the bottom of  my personal list but lo and behold the  credits have just rolled on my first  replay in seven years and I'm eating my  words here is a remaster that doesn't  confirm my vision of the past but up  ends it completely many of my original  frustrations remain yes but the  franchise is different today than it  once was  it's bigger more complicated and my  relation to it has evolved as is often  the result of time context and  resounding personal improvement my view  of Assassin's Creed 3 has changed now I  can see it for what it is a key turning  point in one of the most pervasive  franchises today first off let's talk  about that intro that six hour prologue  that puts you in the shoes of the  unadvertised Haytham Kenway that opening  chunk that illustrates just how similar  the Templars really are to their  assassin rivals these few chapters  humanize the villainous father figure in  a way the Star Wars prequels only dreamt  of doing as you could probably tell I  adore this introduction but in 2012 I  did not in fact I resented it I resented  it for keeping me from playing Connor  this badass Mohawk assassin with his  tomahawk skills and cover art stoicism I  wanted hate thumbs chapter to end almost  as soon as it began despite the twist  that I had been playing as a Templar all  long but I now see how essential that  intro is it reverberates throughout the  entire game as I finally step into  Connors shoes join the assassin  Brotherhood and become embroiled in the  American Revolution I have a clear  target every step of the way  my Templar enemies aren't just names and  faces they're a guy I saved from the  Redcoats or when I rescued from torture  only to help him exact revenge I even  met one in a Boston pub drunkenly  welcoming me to the new world I may have  done these things as Connors father but  that's the point Connor isn't the only  conduit for player agency in the game  and it helps drive the story home the  templars aren't just friends of haytham  Zoar enemies of Connor's they're my  friends and they're my enemies and this  brings me to Connor himself and if I'm  honest my opinion here has not changed  at times he's endearing at times he's  unbearably boring I love the idea that  he's stuck between worlds unsure of  where he belongs pulled between his  Mohawk tribe the assassin Brotherhood  and both sides of the revolution but the  pacing can be so slipshod and Connors  delivery so bland that much of it falls  flat  I get that his confusion and uncertainty  are meant to further drive home the  moral graves of the assassin Templar  conflict but that doesn't excuse Connors  overall lack of spark I can only point  out two times when I was impressed by  his delivery and in both cases he was  consumed with anger  there's no range in his emotions he's  either at an 11 or a 1 this is also  reflected in ac3 stilted character  movement despite the remasters upgraded  textures Ubisoft Barcelona was working  with dated animations and it shows  phases don't convey the right emotion or  in some cases any emotion at all and  Connors feet can't help but magnetize  toward nearby objects at the slightest  touch at his best Connor is more a man  of action than words but when his  actions don't line up with my intent the  cracks really start to show a c3 7-year  old skeleton notwithstanding Connor  remains one of the more tragic figures  in a franchise rife with them throughout  most of the story he's told that he's a  messenger and little more not to dive  too deep into the zany modern-day  storyline of the franchise but basically  you play as Connor so the modern-day  Desmond  reliving his ancestral memories can find  the key to stopping the end of the world  much of Assassin's Creed 3 revolves  around the potential for one person to  make a difference when forces on every  side are set against him this conflict  leads him to kill not just his father  but also his best friend Connor may  falter in his delivery but Ubisoft scrip  places him in a conflicted dramatic spot  if you anticipated Assassin's Creed 3 as  eagerly as I did in 2012 you may have  also worried about the musical direction  of the numerous changes to staff and  direction following the Ezio trilogy one  of the more notable departures was that  of Jesper Kyd the composer responsible  for the musical identity of the first  four games he helped shape each  atmosphere with his period piece  ensembles to lose him was a major blow  to the franchise but upon returning I  can't help but appreciate the confident  quiet ac3 has there are occasional  orchestral touches as you explore the  landscape but they're subtle restrained  the wind is muffled in the underground  tunnels and the frontiers pulled  straight from a Robert Frost poem  I can't say whether the sparse  soundtrack is a result of kids departure  or a decision to just let the wilderness  breathe on its own I just know there's a  peaceful quality to AC threes more  pastoral environments which reminds me  that despite criticisms following early  trailers that Assassin's Creed 3 would  be pro-american the game doesn't  actually romanticize anything about the  revolution let alone pick a side it does  however romanticize of the frontier this  virgin land Connors tribe has sworn to  protect Assassin's Creed 3 used the  frontier to completely revamp the flow  of the franchise's parkour altai ear and  Ezio spent most of their time in urban  environments clambering along rooftops  and over dense crowds in ac3 though  Connor darts through the forest canopy  stalking his prey among natural  formations oddly enough I'm reminded of  the way Metal Gear Solid 3 snake year  brought the series stealth formula to a  more open environment it stressed  cooperation with the wilderness and  reliance on organic materials Conners  adventure is less focused on survival  than naked snakes was but it was a  drastic change regardless and it opened  up new avenues for future Assassin's  Creed settings last year in my video  about the franchise's evolution I used a  quote from Jade Raymond creator of the  Assassin's Creed series founder of EA  motive and recently head of Google's new  stadiums and entertainment to name a few  of her many many accolades in an  interview with The Guardian though she  described the series in a way that sums  it up really well and I still haven't  found a better quote since she called  assassin's Creed a sandbox for creatives  basically it's this thing that creators  can mold into their own vision and after  my recent playthrough unconvinced  Assassin's Creed 3 is one of the key  turning points in the ever-evolving  series it brought in the scope and  brought the formula to new environments  it introduced the beloved naval combat  and paved the way for Assassin's Creed  rogues Templar protagonists for better  or worse it took the kitchen sink  approach with its open-world activities  making Brotherhood already bursting at  the seams with things to do look sparse  by comparison to use Raymond's words it  was a sandbox for creatives and it helps  another decade of sandboxes to come so  it's all the more astonishing to recall  that before its global success  Assassin's Creed was originally planned  as a trilogy ac3 closed out Desmond's  story and it was supposed to bring  finality to the end of the world arc and  for all intents and purposes remain the  last numbered entry in the Assassin's  Creed franchise as Raymond said in the  same interview even she didn't plan on  more than three titles but despite any  initial intent for a singular trilogy  Ubisoft faced the difficult task of  extending a narrative that was  originally supposed to bring closure  much like its protagonists torn between  the worlds of the Mohawk the assassins  and the warring colonists Assassin's  Creed 3 had to become a conduit for  something bigger like Connor ac3 had to  find its place between conflicting ideas  but it also had to establish an identity  of its own it has an age well in many  respects but it's remaster has ignited  my appreciation for what I previously  deemed a mediocre title and as far as  remasters go I'd call that a success
 

